## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize range(p, i-1) queries to O(1) per transition?
- How would you adapt for 1-indexed arrays or allow at most k segments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a 3D DP on a grid with an exact count constraint and path reconstruction, a realistic routing/optimization pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) with 3 dimensions
- Exact-count handling via -inf initialization
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space optimizations via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, boost, K){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const neg = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(neg)));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  const b00 = boost[0][0] ? 1 : 0;
  dp[0][0][b00] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for (let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for (let t=0;t<=K;t++){
        const add = boost[i][j] ? 1 : 0;
        if (i===0 && j===0) continue;
        const candFromTop = i>0 ? dp[i-1][j][t - add] : neg;
        const candFromLeft = j>0 ? dp[i][j-1][t - add] : neg;
        const bestPrev = Math.max(candFromTop, candFromLeft);
        if (bestPrev > neg/2) {
          dp[i][j][t] = bestPrev + grid[i][j];
          par[i][j][t] = candFromTop >= candFromLeft ? ['top'] : ['left'];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n-1][m-1][K], path: []};
}
```

